(218744, 12101, 3575, '<td valign="top">Sonja, your b&amp;w work is so beautiful. This is such a wonderful, well captured of these future ballerinas. I especially like the little girl on the end who looks like it''s time for a potty break.</td>', 'Sonja, your b&w work is so beautiful. This is such a wonderful, well captured of these future ballerinas. I especially like the little girl on the end who looks like it''s time for a potty break.', '2003-02-18 13:55:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(218771, 12238, 3599, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>Well John, another fine photo from you here.  There''s not much for me to say here except I think the right side was cropped too much.  Even if it wasn''t as much the subject I would have liked to see the edge of the staircase.  Why?  Because when my eyes follow the railing it just stopped at the border.  It might be more visually amusing to follow the rail up with your eyes.  I hope that made sense.<br/>I really like the use of B&amp;W here and the contrast as well.  I wish there was more I could add but this is a very good shot that needs little improvement.  Look forward to seeing your photos in future challenges.  If you have any questions feel free to drop me an e-mail.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club. Well John, another fine photo from you here.  There''s not much for me to say here except I think the right side was cropped too much.  Even if it wasn''t as much the subject I would have liked to see the edge of the staircase.  Why?  Because when my eyes follow the railing it just stopped at the border.  It might be more visually amusing to follow the rail up with your eyes.  I hope that made sense.I really like the use of B&W here and the contrast as well.  I wish there was more I could add but this is a very good shot that needs little improvement.  Look forward to seeing your photos in future challenges.  If you have any questions feel free to drop me an e-mail.', '2003-02-18 16:48:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(218841, 12235, 675, '<td valign="top">critique club comment<br/>2 weeks in a row critiquing a magnetic9999 photo. this will be tough because it''s so good.<br/> <br/>Composition<br/> <br/>Your choice of angle is very refreshing after seeing how a lot of photos were overdone.  It is believable that this could actually be a line of sight for someone looking up at this pillar.  The only problem mentioned below and the only problem I really have is the imperfection on the left pillar.  As I look at the picture, my eyes conintually get drawn back to that point, which is a shame.  The choice of using shadows in your composition is also refreshing and does give a sense of grandeur to the pillars.  Excellent job.<br/> <br/>Technical<br/> <br/>Once again, you have created a technically sound photo. DOF is amazing and sharpness is as good as it gets.  The real eye catcher in this photo for me is the choice of duotone.  The color creates an infrared feel to it with the dark sky and bright pillars. This was a great choice and created a great effect.  Once again excellent job.<br/> <br/>Overall<br/> <br/>Very visually stunning and pleasing picture. I''m sorry that I can''t find much fault for you to correct ;).  Keep up the good work<br/></td>', 'critique club comment2 weeks in a row critiquing a magnetic9999 photo. this will be tough because it''s so good. Composition Your choice of angle is very refreshing after seeing how a lot of photos were overdone.  It is believable that this could actually be a line of sight for someone looking up at this pillar.  The only problem mentioned below and the only problem I really have is the imperfection on the left pillar.  As I look at the picture, my eyes conintually get drawn back to that point, which is a shame.  The choice of using shadows in your composition is also refreshing and does give a sense of grandeur to the pillars.  Excellent job. Technical Once again, you have created a technically sound photo. DOF is amazing and sharpness is as good as it gets.  The real eye catcher in this photo for me is the choice of duotone.  The color creates an infrared feel to it with the dark sky and bright pillars. This was a great choice and created a great effect.  Once again excellent job. Overall Very visually stunning and pleasing picture. I''m sorry that I can''t find much fault for you to correct ;).  Keep up the good work', '2003-02-18 19:27:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(218988, 12310, 255, '<td valign="top"><b>CC</b> <br/> <br/>This is great--i remember it really jumped out at me during voting! it is driving me a bit crazy trying to figure out exactly what you did tho--maybe a reflection of a mirror image thru a curved (wine?) glass? i dunno! very cool effect! <br/> <br/>as far as the pic itself goes--i love the stark color scheme. the dark background really shows off the lighter tones of the cigarette. i wonder if the effect might have been more pronounced with a more solid background? i kinda feel like i am looking at a piece of construction paper. that might have worked with another subject, but i dont think it does justice to the patterns in the cigarette filter and paper. also, i think the uncenteredness is interesting, but i think a less tight crop on the top and bottom might do it more justice. <br/> <br/>over all i like the general feel of the photograph. the lighting is very cool --i think the overexposed whites really set off the neat lens work (or however you did it!) nice work! :)</td>', 'CC  This is great--i remember it really jumped out at me during voting! it is driving me a bit crazy trying to figure out exactly what you did tho--maybe a reflection of a mirror image thru a curved (wine?) glass? i dunno! very cool effect!  as far as the pic itself goes--i love the stark color scheme. the dark background really shows off the lighter tones of the cigarette. i wonder if the effect might have been more pronounced with a more solid background? i kinda feel like i am looking at a piece of construction paper. that might have worked with another subject, but i dont think it does justice to the patterns in the cigarette filter and paper. also, i think the uncenteredness is interesting, but i think a less tight crop on the top and bottom might do it more justice.  over all i like the general feel of the photograph. the lighting is very cool --i think the overexposed whites really set off the neat lens work (or however you did it!) nice work! :)', '2003-02-19 02:56:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(219130, 12290, 1041, '<td valign="top">Wow ... thanks everyone - this is my first photo where my lowest vote is a 4!  That totally threw off my score prediction :-P  Please click <a href="//www.langpage.com/dpchallenge/perspective/pages/original.html" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">here</a> to see the photo straight out of the camera.</td>', 'Wow ... thanks everyone - this is my first photo where my lowest vote is a 4!  That totally threw off my score prediction :-P  Please click here to see the photo straight out of the camera.', '2003-02-19 11:54:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(219131, 12118, 1041, '<td valign="top">I''m sorry I didn''t get to vote in this challenge, but your''s was one of my favorites.  Congrats on a very well-deserved win. :)</td>', 'I''m sorry I didn''t get to vote in this challenge, but your''s was one of my favorites.  Congrats on a very well-deserved win. :)', '2003-02-19 11:55:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(219133, 12268, 1041, '<td valign="top">I didn''t get to vote on this challenge, but I really liked those warm colors.  Congrats on your well-deserved win :)</td>', 'I didn''t get to vote on this challenge, but I really liked those warm colors.  Congrats on your well-deserved win :)', '2003-02-19 11:55:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(219134, 12176, 1041, '<td valign="top">Congrats on the ribbon, Pep! :)</td>', 'Congrats on the ribbon, Pep! :)', '2003-02-19 11:56:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(219138, 12299, 1041, '<td valign="top">Oh, man, I could''ve sworn this one would''ve been the top winner.  An absolutely awesome shot, congrats! :)</td>', 'Oh, man, I could''ve sworn this one would''ve been the top winner.  An absolutely awesome shot, congrats! :)', '2003-02-19 11:59:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(219145, 12290, 5898, '<td valign="top">I am a purest when it comes to photography. I don''t even like to crop my pictures. So when I read your personal comments I was a little disappointed in how much altering of the origional picture you had done. I checked out the origional photo and that one I would not have graded as high. This of course is a personal opinion. So leaving my prejudices out of this I shall comment. I am really big into using the elements of design to create fantastic photographs. Your point of view, perspective, lines, shapes and forms, pattern, reflection,and texture,makes this picture come alive. The rich blues vs the gray stones make for a dramatic contrast. The curvature of the columns gives the picture some depth. Very sharp image and excellent cropping. What I appreciate the most is the fact that you have an eye for the beauty that surrounds us all and then you were able to capture it using the camera. Great job! JOHN GILL</td>', 'I am a purest when it comes to photography. I don''t even like to crop my pictures. So when I read your personal comments I was a little disappointed in how much altering of the origional picture you had done. I checked out the origional photo and that one I would not have graded as high. This of course is a personal opinion. So leaving my prejudices out of this I shall comment. I am really big into using the elements of design to create fantastic photographs. Your point of view, perspective, lines, shapes and forms, pattern, reflection,and texture,makes this picture come alive. The rich blues vs the gray stones make for a dramatic contrast. The curvature of the columns gives the picture some depth. Very sharp image and excellent cropping. What I appreciate the most is the fact that you have an eye for the beauty that surrounds us all and then you were able to capture it using the camera. Great job! JOHN GILL', '2003-02-19 12:19:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(219287, 12202, 5898, '<td valign="top">There are two things I really like about this picture. First the contrast of the rectangular rail and the circular wall. Second, the texture of the broken railing and the smoothness of the back walls. The point of view you chose and your cropping works to your advantage. Great depth of field, focus, and going to black and white add to the high quality of this work. I can''t recall the score I gave you but I know it was high. Keep up the great work, John Gill</td>', 'There are two things I really like about this picture. First the contrast of the rectangular rail and the circular wall. Second, the texture of the broken railing and the smoothness of the back walls. The point of view you chose and your cropping works to your advantage. Great depth of field, focus, and going to black and white add to the high quality of this work. I can''t recall the score I gave you but I know it was high. Keep up the great work, John Gill', '2003-02-19 15:15:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(219299, 12177, 5898, '<td valign="top">Two things work really well for this photo. Point of View and the great texture at the base of the tree. I''m glad to see you cropped the picture because I''m sure the composition presented here is better because it allows viewers to finish the image of the tree. This shot is very over used, but it is probably one of the better ones I''ve seen. Good Job, John Gill. Ps. I understand the problems with the weather, I waited 3 days to get the kind of lighting I wanted and finally gave up. But it does make a difference.</td>', 'Two things work really well for this photo. Point of View and the great texture at the base of the tree. I''m glad to see you cropped the picture because I''m sure the composition presented here is better because it allows viewers to finish the image of the tree. This shot is very over used, but it is probably one of the better ones I''ve seen. Good Job, John Gill. Ps. I understand the problems with the weather, I waited 3 days to get the kind of lighting I wanted and finally gave up. But it does make a difference.', '2003-02-19 15:21:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(219326, 12302, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Tarbini...<br/> <br/>This is a beautiful landscape shot... As I stated in my earlier comment, I believe the colors here are extraordinary... great work with that for sure :)  I believe that you may have intended your ''perspective'' in this shot to be the livestock giving perspective to the size of the windmills... That works VERY well here.  I can''t really offer you any suggestions for improvement at all... I wouldn''t have changed anything about the photo... <br/> <br/>Great shot!  Keep up the good work :)<br/> <br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/> <br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Tarbini... This is a beautiful landscape shot... As I stated in my earlier comment, I believe the colors here are extraordinary... great work with that for sure :)  I believe that you may have intended your ''perspective'' in this shot to be the livestock giving perspective to the size of the windmills... That works VERY well here.  I can''t really offer you any suggestions for improvement at all... I wouldn''t have changed anything about the photo...  Great shot!  Keep up the good work :)  John Setzler  ', '2003-02-19 15:48:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(219449, 12236, 1143, '<td valign="top">HI! It looks like you live in a nicely restored old building. I love places like that. <br/> <br/>Composition: The wood railing and ironwork is really cool. The stairs themselves just aren''t too interesting. I don''t know how you would isolate the rail and iron more though ... what happens if you shoot up from that lower landing? Is there some way to compose it against a solid wall or ceiling? I think I''d rather see it more as an architectural detail shot - rather abstract - than a stairwell shot. I sort of like the window in that it matches the railing, but it puts a little too much interest in that top part of the shot.<br/> <br/>Lighting is good. The shellac on the end of the railing is obviously very shiny ... You can hold up some sort of paper or diffusing material to block direct light on problem areas like that.<br/> <br/>Focus: I guess you tried to use a shallow dof to emphasize the perspective, but I''m not sure it works with this shot. There just isn''t enough difference between the near and far. I''d rather see it all in focus or the front in focus and the back blurred a lot more than it is.<br/> <br/>Yes, this shot fits the challenge. Technically, it has a few glitches but nothing major. I think the biggest problem was just composition and subject. If the wood and ironwork is what drew you to this subject then that is what you should try to really show in the shot. Having the stairs and window just makes it a little too cluttered IMO. There are too many lines going too many different directions. <br/> <br/>Your score was decent though, and it''s obvious that you are keeping your eyes open. Good luck in the future challenges.</td>', 'HI! It looks like you live in a nicely restored old building. I love places like that.  Composition: The wood railing and ironwork is really cool. The stairs themselves just aren''t too interesting. I don''t know how you would isolate the rail and iron more though ... what happens if you shoot up from that lower landing? Is there some way to compose it against a solid wall or ceiling? I think I''d rather see it more as an architectural detail shot - rather abstract - than a stairwell shot. I sort of like the window in that it matches the railing, but it puts a little too much interest in that top part of the shot. Lighting is good. The shellac on the end of the railing is obviously very shiny ... You can hold up some sort of paper or diffusing material to block direct light on problem areas like that. Focus: I guess you tried to use a shallow dof to emphasize the perspective, but I''m not sure it works with this shot. There just isn''t enough difference between the near and far. I''d rather see it all in focus or the front in focus and the back blurred a lot more than it is. Yes, this shot fits the challenge. Technically, it has a few glitches but nothing major. I think the biggest problem was just composition and subject. If the wood and ironwork is what drew you to this subject then that is what you should try to really show in the shot. Having the stairs and window just makes it a little too cluttered IMO. There are too many lines going too many different directions.  Your score was decent though, and it''s obvious that you are keeping your eyes open. Good luck in the future challenges.', '2003-02-19 18:45:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(219476, 12084, 6872, '<td valign="top">Great shot.  I really like it a lot.  I live in Boston for college and I think this is one of the nicest shots of Boston that I have seen.</td>', 'Great shot.  I really like it a lot.  I live in Boston for college and I think this is one of the nicest shots of Boston that I have seen.', '2003-02-19 19:51:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(219568, 12216, 1280, '<td valign="top">Greetins from the Critique Club!<br/>This meets the challenge nicely and the coloring is nice.  The red adds a little something extra to it.  <br/>I like the perspective you''ve chosen, although I know there would also have been other options, and perhaps would have been even more appealing.  Although there is nothing wrong with the perspective you''ve chosen, I think if the image indicated what the instrument is, it would have added to the appeal in general.<br/>The focus isn''t tack sharp any where.  The shallow dof would be fine, if there was one area that was very sharp as a focal point. <br/>The over all coloring is warm and I like the way the silver-toned metal strings contrast with all the warmth. I like the way the textures contrast too.<br/>Hope to see more of your work.<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce</td>', 'Greetins from the Critique Club!This meets the challenge nicely and the coloring is nice.  The red adds a little something extra to it.  I like the perspective you''ve chosen, although I know there would also have been other options, and perhaps would have been even more appealing.  Although there is nothing wrong with the perspective you''ve chosen, I think if the image indicated what the instrument is, it would have added to the appeal in general.The focus isn''t tack sharp any where.  The shallow dof would be fine, if there was one area that was very sharp as a focal point. The over all coloring is warm and I like the way the silver-toned metal strings contrast with all the warmth. I like the way the textures contrast too.Hope to see more of your work.Regards, Grayce', '2003-02-19 21:32:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(219588, 12195, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comments by Grayce<br/>Wow!  What an unusual subject!  This is quite original considering it is one of a kind.  <br/>IT''s somewhat exciting because my heart skips a beat over concern that a fire may catch...lol  This works because it is so unexpected.<br/>Technically speaking, the exposure is good, showing detail in the cardboard box.  The greyscale works fine here and it is well composed.  <br/>As an image it doesn''t stand out as a fine work of art, but it is somewhat effective as I previously mentioned, therefore it is successful.<br/> <br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce</td>', 'Critique Club Comments by GrayceWow!  What an unusual subject!  This is quite original considering it is one of a kind.  IT''s somewhat exciting because my heart skips a beat over concern that a fire may catch...lol  This works because it is so unexpected.Technically speaking, the exposure is good, showing detail in the cardboard box.  The greyscale works fine here and it is well composed.  As an image it doesn''t stand out as a fine work of art, but it is somewhat effective as I previously mentioned, therefore it is successful. Regards, Grayce', '2003-02-19 21:49:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(219646, 12214, 327, '<td valign="top"><b>CC for Konador</b> <br/>Ha! Funny I got your shot of ''spindles'' since I did basically the same shot. Must be Karma!<br/>Fits The Challenge-Yes.<br/>Composition-Very nice <br/>Background-n/a <br/>Digital Processing-Nicely done shallow dof with interesting color.<br/>My Opinion-I like your blue tint ....makes the whole shot interesting, softer with added drama. I think like my shot yours suffered from the first spindle. &lt;?&gt; Mine also had the added problem of a sever angle......(I thought it was cool at the time :0|)I like the texture of the wood and the fact that the stairway seems to fade away. Nice touch. <br/>Good work.<br/>p.s. your title was perfect.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-02-19 23:04:04.</i></td>', 'CC for Konador Ha! Funny I got your shot of ''spindles'' since I did basically the same shot. Must be Karma!Fits The Challenge-Yes.Composition-Very nice Background-n/a Digital Processing-Nicely done shallow dof with interesting color.My Opinion-I like your blue tint ....makes the whole shot interesting, softer with added drama. I think like my shot yours suffered from the first spindle. <?> Mine also had the added problem of a sever angle......(I thought it was cool at the time :0|)I like the texture of the wood and the fact that the stairway seems to fade away. Nice touch. Good work.p.s. your title was perfect.', '2003-02-19 23:02:58.000000', '2003-02-19 23:04:04.000000', 0),
(219899, 12078, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Gallatin...<br/> <br/>This is an excellent landscape photo.  The perspective aspect of the river works very well also.  I am really at a loss for what to tell you on this particular photo for improvement possibilities.  I like what I see and I don''t have much to offer.<br/> <br/>As I look at the photo, I believe that you may be standing on a bridge or waklway above the river.  I''m not sure if there would have been a way to get the camera closer to water level or not, but sometimes that can produce a nice effect in a landscape shot of this nature as well.  <br/> <br/>This park seems like it would be full of great photo opportunities... I hope you enjoyed your visit there and took lots of photos :)<br/> <br/>Keep up the great work :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Gallatin... This is an excellent landscape photo.  The perspective aspect of the river works very well also.  I am really at a loss for what to tell you on this particular photo for improvement possibilities.  I like what I see and I don''t have much to offer. As I look at the photo, I believe that you may be standing on a bridge or waklway above the river.  I''m not sure if there would have been a way to get the camera closer to water level or not, but sometimes that can produce a nice effect in a landscape shot of this nature as well.   This park seems like it would be full of great photo opportunities... I hope you enjoyed your visit there and took lots of photos :) Keep up the great work :) John Setzler', '2003-02-20 08:56:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(220015, 12176, 3326, '<td valign="top">Thanks to all. I really apreciate your comments. This is a great site with a great comunity.<br/> <br/><a href="//www.pbase.com/pepventosa" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.pbase.com/pepventosa</a></td>', 'Thanks to all. I really apreciate your comments. This is a great site with a great comunity. //www.pbase.com/pepventosa', '2003-02-20 13:50:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(220023, 12305, 327, '<td valign="top"><b>CC:</b>Hello welcher!<br/>Neat underwater shot. I had a Nikon under water camera that I took to Cozumel, Mexico. Deep sea diving and snorkeling. Most of the shots were not award winners but some of them I''m proud of. I hope you get to use your new housing in the ocean.<br/>Fits The Challenge-Yes <br/>Composition-Very neat perspective looking up to the surface. <br/>Background-Good, fits the shot and adds to the story. <br/>Digital Processing- A slight moiré pattern.<br/>My Opinion-This shot like a lot of your work is creative and inventive. You''ve got a unique flair to you photos thats fun, interesting and different from the average. I think <b>You''re My Pal</b> did well and I''m sure you''ll be back with more ''water'' shots.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-02-20 14:35:45.</i></td>', 'CC:Hello welcher!Neat underwater shot. I had a Nikon under water camera that I took to Cozumel, Mexico. Deep sea diving and snorkeling. Most of the shots were not award winners but some of them I''m proud of. I hope you get to use your new housing in the ocean.Fits The Challenge-Yes Composition-Very neat perspective looking up to the surface. Background-Good, fits the shot and adds to the story. Digital Processing- A slight moiré pattern.My Opinion-This shot like a lot of your work is creative and inventive. You''ve got a unique flair to you photos thats fun, interesting and different from the average. I think You''re My Pal did well and I''m sure you''ll be back with more ''water'' shots.', '2003-02-20 14:35:12.000000', '2003-02-20 14:35:45.000000', 0),
(220078, 12296, 1280, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>The subject is a good one, and popular with many.  It''s sort of timeless, with little change over the years.  So at the same time it is current and nostalgic.<br/>The depth of field seems to work just fine, although I''d like to see the whole a little sharper. <br/>All the wood within the tracks is wonderfully textural and adds much dimension to the image.  <br/>I like the crop but, personally, not the angle.  I understand it is a matter of personal taste though, and others really like it. <br/>Overall a decent image that could be presented in several different ways.  <br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubThe subject is a good one, and popular with many.  It''s sort of timeless, with little change over the years.  So at the same time it is current and nostalgic.The depth of field seems to work just fine, although I''d like to see the whole a little sharper. All the wood within the tracks is wonderfully textural and adds much dimension to the image.  I like the crop but, personally, not the angle.  I understand it is a matter of personal taste though, and others really like it. Overall a decent image that could be presented in several different ways.  Regards, Grayce', '2003-02-20 16:51:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(220091, 12109, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comments by Grayce<br/>The  composition of this is very good, but the crop is a little too close to the head.  I''d like to see a little room there.<br/>Color saturation, and exposure are both good..<br/>The subject is a little mysterious.  Makes me wonder what this is all about, and why is it covered up?  For that reason it draws the viewer in.<br/>I don''t find this very appealing, so I''m not really sure what else to say. I see others are quite fond of it, so it really is a matter of taste.<br/>Just keep on enjoying your photography.<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce</td>', 'Critique Club Comments by GrayceThe  composition of this is very good, but the crop is a little too close to the head.  I''d like to see a little room there.Color saturation, and exposure are both good..The subject is a little mysterious.  Makes me wonder what this is all about, and why is it covered up?  For that reason it draws the viewer in.I don''t find this very appealing, so I''m not really sure what else to say. I see others are quite fond of it, so it really is a matter of taste.Just keep on enjoying your photography.Regards, Grayce', '2003-02-20 17:18:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(220274, 12143, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION<br/>The strong vertical motion really pulls the viewer into this one.  The foremost pillars frame the shot nicely, and it works being symmetrical. (Sometimes shots that seem to be intended to be symmetrical, are just a touch off adn it is difficult to tell if it was intentional or not.)  <br/> <br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE<br/>You have done well to have the front pillars focused, and having focus throughout the picture, though the grass does seem a little fuzzy.  Maybe you could have used a slightly larger aperture number, slowed down the shutter a touch, and gotten more focus and detail shown.  Also, itis a little "drab" in that the pillars are all the same color (I know, that''s out of your control) and the grass as well.  I would suggest boosting the contrast and converting it to black and white.  If you really wanted color, maybe saturating the grass (not a lot you can do with Carolina grass in February!)  <br/> <br/> <br/>OVERALL EFFECT<br/>Great subject for the challenge.  I think the picture would have had a little more oomph if the colors were a little more bold, or if there were some more contrast.  Too bad there wasn''t a bright red sign at the end or something to really be a focus spot.</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat COMPOSITIONThe strong vertical motion really pulls the viewer into this one.  The foremost pillars frame the shot nicely, and it works being symmetrical. (Sometimes shots that seem to be intended to be symmetrical, are just a touch off adn it is difficult to tell if it was intentional or not.)    TECHNIQUEYou have done well to have the front pillars focused, and having focus throughout the picture, though the grass does seem a little fuzzy.  Maybe you could have used a slightly larger aperture number, slowed down the shutter a touch, and gotten more focus and detail shown.  Also, itis a little "drab" in that the pillars are all the same color (I know, that''s out of your control) and the grass as well.  I would suggest boosting the contrast and converting it to black and white.  If you really wanted color, maybe saturating the grass (not a lot you can do with Carolina grass in February!)    OVERALL EFFECTGreat subject for the challenge.  I think the picture would have had a little more oomph if the colors were a little more bold, or if there were some more contrast.  Too bad there wasn''t a bright red sign at the end or something to really be a focus spot.', '2003-02-20 23:19:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(220286, 12141, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION<br/>Great choice of subject matter.  I really like the flow of the diagonal lines, and how they set up contrasts by going in different directions.  The brightness of the top steps really grabs the viewer''s eyes, so it may have been more effective to have less of them and more of the bottom steps, then the eyes would travel through the picture more easily.<br/> <br/> <br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE<br/>The focus is good, and the lighting overall establishes some nice tonal contrast.  I think the upper part is a bit bright, but it would be difficult to tone that down without losing some in the lower sections.  You have captured the textures of the steps very well.<br/> <br/> <br/>OVERALL EFFECT<br/>I love the optical illusion of this one.  It is confounding enough to make you look at it for a few seconds more, then it just pops out at you.  It looks so 2-dimensional, and 3-dimensional, at the same time.   Overall, very nicely done.</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat COMPOSITIONGreat choice of subject matter.  I really like the flow of the diagonal lines, and how they set up contrasts by going in different directions.  The brightness of the top steps really grabs the viewer''s eyes, so it may have been more effective to have less of them and more of the bottom steps, then the eyes would travel through the picture more easily.   TECHNIQUEThe focus is good, and the lighting overall establishes some nice tonal contrast.  I think the upper part is a bit bright, but it would be difficult to tone that down without losing some in the lower sections.  You have captured the textures of the steps very well.  OVERALL EFFECTI love the optical illusion of this one.  It is confounding enough to make you look at it for a few seconds more, then it just pops out at you.  It looks so 2-dimensional, and 3-dimensional, at the same time.   Overall, very nicely done.', '2003-02-20 23:42:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(220293, 12176, 3280, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club };-) </b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts </b> <br/>awesome shot, meets the challenge, I like the Sepia<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content </b> <br/>A great perspective shot, the combination of the trees and buildings is really great. I like the choice of Sepia as I feel it makes you focus more on the tree.<br/> <br/><b>Background</b> <br/>About the only thing you could have improved here was to maybe take the sunlight off of the top of the building on the right.  I don''t think that was possible but I HAD to come up with some improvement right?<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work - Technical</b> <br/>Focus seems good and contrast is excellent for the difference in light and shadow you have here.  The contrast is what may have really made it a killer shot.<br/> <br/><b>Digital Processing - Technical </b> <br/>I like the border, especially the matching color.<br/> <br/><b>Fits The Challenge </b> <br/>Definitely fits the challenge well. <br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo </b> <br/>I was so happy to see you get a ribbon this week. It is an awesome shot that had super creativity and an eye for perspective! Great job, and keep up the good work. <br/> <br/> <br/>I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.<br/><b>DougPaz</b> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club };-)   Initial thoughts  awesome shot, meets the challenge, I like the Sepia Composition/ Content  A great perspective shot, the combination of the trees and buildings is really great. I like the choice of Sepia as I feel it makes you focus more on the tree. Background About the only thing you could have improved here was to maybe take the sunlight off of the top of the building on the right.  I don''t think that was possible but I HAD to come up with some improvement right? Camera Work - Technical Focus seems good and contrast is excellent for the difference in light and shadow you have here.  The contrast is what may have really made it a killer shot. Digital Processing - Technical  I like the border, especially the matching color. Fits The Challenge  Definitely fits the challenge well.  My Opinion On The Photo  I was so happy to see you get a ribbon this week. It is an awesome shot that had super creativity and an eye for perspective! Great job, and keep up the good work.   I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.DougPaz ', '2003-02-21 00:00:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(220301, 12118, 674, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>Firstly double congratulations on your win and your great perspective image, this is one I would like hanging on my wall!<br/>Composition : Good<br/>Exposure: You have made an excellent job of what must been rather challenging winter light conditions!<br/>Some suggestions<br/>What did it look like in black and white instead of sepia? I was going to suggest cropping a bit more but you would lose some of those beautiful reflections and the way that lines lead you into the image!<br/>Great image!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubFirstly double congratulations on your win and your great perspective image, this is one I would like hanging on my wall!Composition : GoodExposure: You have made an excellent job of what must been rather challenging winter light conditions!Some suggestionsWhat did it look like in black and white instead of sepia? I was going to suggest cropping a bit more but you would lose some of those beautiful reflections and the way that lines lead you into the image!Great image!', '2003-02-21 00:17:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(220437, 12301, 674, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>Composition <br/>The subject is easily identifiable as the strange ice formations that immediately grab ones attention. But the horizon line is not level and I dont quite get the perspective aspect.<br/>Focus and exposure are excellent, as are Contrast &amp; Color: <br/>Suggestions for improvement<br/>A more level horizon</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubComposition The subject is easily identifiable as the strange ice formations that immediately grab ones attention. But the horizon line is not level and I dont quite get the perspective aspect.Focus and exposure are excellent, as are Contrast & Color: Suggestions for improvementA more level horizon', '2003-02-21 08:26:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(220444, 12101, 674, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>Composition <br/>This is beautifully composed shot the eye is led right into the image to the little lass at the end! I hope someone went to her rescue!And the various elements of the photo are well balanced within the frame<br/>Exposure: Contrast &amp; Color, and focus are spot on!<br/> <br/>Suggestions for improvement<br/>Perhaps play with the brightness and contrast and see if you can get even more tonality into the scene. And Please do away with the pink border it is a detraction and distraction from a lovely photo!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubComposition This is beautifully composed shot the eye is led right into the image to the little lass at the end! I hope someone went to her rescue!And the various elements of the photo are well balanced within the frameExposure: Contrast & Color, and focus are spot on! Suggestions for improvementPerhaps play with the brightness and contrast and see if you can get even more tonality into the scene. And Please do away with the pink border it is a detraction and distraction from a lovely photo!', '2003-02-21 08:36:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(220476, 12282, 5898, '<td valign="top">There are some very positive points to this image. The perspective, pattern, point of view, shapes and fibrant colors make for a quality photograph. What distracts me and why I graded it a little lower than your final score was the large dark area in the mindle of the picture. I feel that area dominates the picture and detracts from all the positive things going on in the rest of your photograph. John Gill</td>', 'There are some very positive points to this image. The perspective, pattern, point of view, shapes and fibrant colors make for a quality photograph. What distracts me and why I graded it a little lower than your final score was the large dark area in the mindle of the picture. I feel that area dominates the picture and detracts from all the positive things going on in the rest of your photograph. John Gill', '2003-02-21 10:06:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(220601, 12189, 5898, '<td valign="top">Very well composed, great lighting that casts some nice shadows. The perspective and the curving lines makes this image one of the stronger ones I''ve seen. I really like the color/everything...Great, great image. You should have received a much higher grade. JOHN GILL</td>', 'Very well composed, great lighting that casts some nice shadows. The perspective and the curving lines makes this image one of the stronger ones I''ve seen. I really like the color/everything...Great, great image. You should have received a much higher grade. JOHN GILL', '2003-02-21 15:09:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(220619, 12056, 5898, '<td valign="top">I find nothing wrong with the angle or the cropping of your picture. Perhaps to much sharpening, but even then it makes for a very unique image. I especially like the two different perspectives, the tighter one of the left trees and the more spread out perspective of the right hand trees. The lines created by all the branches keeps the eye moving and the mind ingaged. Definitely a much higher quality picture than the score you received. Keep up the great work. John Gill</td>', 'I find nothing wrong with the angle or the cropping of your picture. Perhaps to much sharpening, but even then it makes for a very unique image. I especially like the two different perspectives, the tighter one of the left trees and the more spread out perspective of the right hand trees. The lines created by all the branches keeps the eye moving and the mind ingaged. Definitely a much higher quality picture than the score you received. Keep up the great work. John Gill', '2003-02-21 15:36:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(220654, 12061, 2279, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club.<br/> <br/>Composition: It seems to me that you have two subjects fighting for attention. Of course the chihuahua is the main subject but the REALLY BRIGHT sign distracts me from the dog.<br/> <br/>Technical: The entire shot is out of focus. I think you would have been better off if you either used a tripod or a faster shutter speed and maybe overexpose it a bit.<br/> <br/>Overall:  I think this would have done much better if the shot was brighter and you got rid of the sign.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-02-21 17:03:41.</i></td>', 'Greetings from the critique club. Composition: It seems to me that you have two subjects fighting for attention. Of course the chihuahua is the main subject but the REALLY BRIGHT sign distracts me from the dog. Technical: The entire shot is out of focus. I think you would have been better off if you either used a tripod or a faster shutter speed and maybe overexpose it a bit. Overall:  I think this would have done much better if the shot was brighter and you got rid of the sign.', '2003-02-21 17:01:34.000000', '2003-02-21 17:03:41.000000', 0),
(220656, 12130, 2279, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club.<br/> <br/>Composition: I really like the angle for this shot. The three colors you chose for this work well too. Including the sun in this was also another added bonus. The blue sky for a background is nice because there are no distractions to take your eyes away from the subject. I think repositioning the shot so the sun was still there but there wasnt so much negative space at the top would have really centered the attention on the main subject.<br/> <br/>Technical: The focus is great. The lens flare is a really big distraction though. <br/> <br/>Overall: Great picture and great composition and colors with the only really big distraction being the lens flare.</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club. Composition: I really like the angle for this shot. The three colors you chose for this work well too. Including the sun in this was also another added bonus. The blue sky for a background is nice because there are no distractions to take your eyes away from the subject. I think repositioning the shot so the sun was still there but there wasnt so much negative space at the top would have really centered the attention on the main subject. Technical: The focus is great. The lens flare is a really big distraction though.  Overall: Great picture and great composition and colors with the only really big distraction being the lens flare.', '2003-02-21 17:19:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(220667, 12244, 3730, '<td valign="top">...from Critique Club... <br/> <br/>Hi David :)<br/> <br/>FIRST IMPRESSION: <br/>Cool perspective, out of focus<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION: <br/>I like the setup of this shot. You really did capture an interesting perspective. I like how all sides lead to the light right in the middle. This shows how a shot can work even if centered, IMO. :) <br/> <br/>TECHNICAL: <br/>This is where I think you lost most of the votes. The lightingis tricky to get right cause your foreground is fairly dark, but the background if very bright. I''m not sure how you''d shoot this to get the right exposure all around...maybe with a flash to compensate for the dark inside. Also the focus is very soft, I think sharp focus would have helped you alot. :(<br/> <br/>ARTISTIC: <br/>I think the shot is quite interesting even if technically weak. Sure meets the challenge :)<br/> <br/>Cheers and good luck in the future challenges.</td>', '...from Critique Club...  Hi David :) FIRST IMPRESSION: Cool perspective, out of focus COMPOSITION: I like the setup of this shot. You really did capture an interesting perspective. I like how all sides lead to the light right in the middle. This shows how a shot can work even if centered, IMO. :)  TECHNICAL: This is where I think you lost most of the votes. The lightingis tricky to get right cause your foreground is fairly dark, but the background if very bright. I''m not sure how you''d shoot this to get the right exposure all around...maybe with a flash to compensate for the dark inside. Also the focus is very soft, I think sharp focus would have helped you alot. :( ARTISTIC: I think the shot is quite interesting even if technically weak. Sure meets the challenge :) Cheers and good luck in the future challenges.', '2003-02-21 17:59:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(220679, 12076, 3730, '<td valign="top">...from Critique Club... <br/> <br/>Hi Anona :)<br/> <br/>FIRST IMPRESSION: <br/>nice...but that border''s gotta go<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION: <br/>Wonderful composition. I think you should have left it w/out a border, it is way to harsh and distracts from the subject. :) <br/> <br/>TECHNICAL: <br/>lighting is great. I like your selective DOF. nice sharp focus on the flag...great job :) <br/> <br/>ARTISTIC: <br/>It''s just another flag shot...i think that''s why you finished 38th. I''m sure you realize that this is an international website, and with what''s going on in the world you might lose points for a shot of an american flag...it''s just reality.<br/> <br/>Interesting how jmsetzler jumped to the conclusion that you were going to get ''nasty remarks'' when in fact you got NONE... :) <br/> <br/>Cheers and good luck in the future challenges.</td>', '...from Critique Club...  Hi Anona :) FIRST IMPRESSION: nice...but that border''s gotta go COMPOSITION: Wonderful composition. I think you should have left it w/out a border, it is way to harsh and distracts from the subject. :)  TECHNICAL: lighting is great. I like your selective DOF. nice sharp focus on the flag...great job :)  ARTISTIC: It''s just another flag shot...i think that''s why you finished 38th. I''m sure you realize that this is an international website, and with what''s going on in the world you might lose points for a shot of an american flag...it''s just reality. Interesting how jmsetzler jumped to the conclusion that you were going to get ''nasty remarks'' when in fact you got NONE... :)  Cheers and good luck in the future challenges.', '2003-02-21 18:22:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(220687, 12204, 714, '<td valign="top">Great shot Joshua,  I think you did a great job at meeting the challenge and this should be a favorite of yours.  It should have done better.  Keep shooting!</td>', 'Great shot Joshua,  I think you did a great job at meeting the challenge and this should be a favorite of yours.  It should have done better.  Keep shooting!', '2003-02-21 18:53:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(220704, 12165, 2279, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club.<br/> <br/>Composition: This is a very centered photo but I think it works in this case. I think if you removed the two leaves near the top center it would have shown more of the tree and more importantly the perspective of the tree. The brick background is a little distracting but you might not have had any other choice.<br/> <br/>Technical: The bottom of the tree seems out of focus. Using a wider depth of field would have fixed that. Also the colors seem a little drab. Maybe in your photo editor you could have bumped up the contrast or levels a bit.<br/> <br/>Overall: This is a good perspective shot and with a little pre-shot grooming of the tree and a little photo editing would have helped place this shot higher.</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club. Composition: This is a very centered photo but I think it works in this case. I think if you removed the two leaves near the top center it would have shown more of the tree and more importantly the perspective of the tree. The brick background is a little distracting but you might not have had any other choice. Technical: The bottom of the tree seems out of focus. Using a wider depth of field would have fixed that. Also the colors seem a little drab. Maybe in your photo editor you could have bumped up the contrast or levels a bit. Overall: This is a good perspective shot and with a little pre-shot grooming of the tree and a little photo editing would have helped place this shot higher.', '2003-02-21 20:36:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(221165, 12206, 4388, '<td valign="top"><b>Critique Club Critique</b> <br/><u>Composition/Content</u> Nice clean shiney shot with good color, simple but yet with detail. Good tonal balance. Nice geometric lines across the image with stops at each verticle. Nice detail in the reflections.<br/><u>Camera Work/Technical</u> Excellent focus and exposure. The camera angle is what makes this image interesting.<br/><u>My Opinion</u> Can''t fault the image. All aspects are right on. Great contrast and use of black, white and red. Good imagination and execution. Excellent shot.</td>', 'Critique Club Critique Composition/Content Nice clean shiney shot with good color, simple but yet with detail. Good tonal balance. Nice geometric lines across the image with stops at each verticle. Nice detail in the reflections.Camera Work/Technical Excellent focus and exposure. The camera angle is what makes this image interesting.My Opinion Can''t fault the image. All aspects are right on. Great contrast and use of black, white and red. Good imagination and execution. Excellent shot.', '2003-02-22 15:41:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(221175, 12304, 2279, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Critique:<br/> <br/>Composition: I think the centered tower works great for this shot although another approach you could try would be having the tower start at one of the bottom corners and maybe cut across the shot at an angle. There is a lot of empty space in this picture so the cloud really does help a lot.<br/> <br/>Technical: Not much to say here. Lighting is great, focus is nice and sharp.<br/> <br/>Overall: Well you met the challenge and did a really good job with the shot. Maybe just a little tighter crop would have raised your score a few points.</td>', 'Critique Club Critique: Composition: I think the centered tower works great for this shot although another approach you could try would be having the tower start at one of the bottom corners and maybe cut across the shot at an angle. There is a lot of empty space in this picture so the cloud really does help a lot. Technical: Not much to say here. Lighting is great, focus is nice and sharp. Overall: Well you met the challenge and did a really good job with the shot. Maybe just a little tighter crop would have raised your score a few points.', '2003-02-22 16:02:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(221176, 12189, 6349, '<td valign="top">very nice picture!  Great color, perspective, and lighting.  You beat me this week, but that''s because I got robbed :P  I''m surprised you didn''t get any 10''s at all.  And a 2?  lol.  who knows.</td>', 'very nice picture!  Great color, perspective, and lighting.  You beat me this week, but that''s because I got robbed :P  I''m surprised you didn''t get any 10''s at all.  And a 2?  lol.  who knows.', '2003-02-22 16:04:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(221209, 12069, 4388, '<td valign="top"><b>Critique Club Critique</b> <br/><u>Composition/Content</u> Good imagination in viewing the farthest handcuff through the near one. Nice and simple image makes it interesting. Both the angle and the perspective combine in a unique way to present this image. Solid white background was a good choice.<br/><u>Lighting</u> Good lighting and contrast.<br/><u>Camera Work/Technical</u> Great use of DOF for effect. Good exposure.<br/><u>My Opinion</u> I like your imagination and expression in this image. I would like to have seen slightly less angle. I also wonder what the image would look like if both handcuffs were in focus. Very nice job.<br/></td>', 'Critique Club Critique Composition/Content Good imagination in viewing the farthest handcuff through the near one. Nice and simple image makes it interesting. Both the angle and the perspective combine in a unique way to present this image. Solid white background was a good choice.Lighting Good lighting and contrast.Camera Work/Technical Great use of DOF for effect. Good exposure.My Opinion I like your imagination and expression in this image. I would like to have seen slightly less angle. I also wonder what the image would look like if both handcuffs were in focus. Very nice job.', '2003-02-22 18:36:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(221390, 12146, 2221, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club, Danny!<br/> <br/>Composition: Very interesting. At a first glance I also would have said, that you should have cropped more from the right side. But then I realised that the patterns on the wall are quite interesting. But there is something about the composition which feels odd. I think using the rule of thirds would improve the photo. The centering doesn''t fit here. I don''t know if it would have been technically possible, but instead of cropping from the right I would like to see more from the left side. Then there also could be seen more of the darker area on the left, which corresponds the the right side.<br/> <br/>Lighting: I guess the red colour comes from a wrong or no white balance setting. I don''t know of that was intentional but I would like it be more "natural". I know it''s a bit silly because for this subject it does not matter if it''s red, yellow, blue or green. But the red just _looks_ like a technical "error". While testing some different crops I also changed the hue a little (+16) which results in a more golden/yellow tone. I think that''s much better.<br/>The "star" effect created by the small aperture is nice.<br/> <br/>Focus: Generally ok. 1/2s exposure time, so you surely used a tripod. I can''t make out where the camera focussed, but the foliage could be a bit sharper.<br/> <br/>Challenge: Met. A deeper tunnel would have had more impact, but it certainly fits to the challenge theme.<br/> <br/>Creativity: Well, not overly creative, but not totally boring either.<br/> <br/>If you have questions just send me an Email. I''ll happily discuss this more.<br/>-Stephan<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club, Danny! Composition: Very interesting. At a first glance I also would have said, that you should have cropped more from the right side. But then I realised that the patterns on the wall are quite interesting. But there is something about the composition which feels odd. I think using the rule of thirds would improve the photo. The centering doesn''t fit here. I don''t know if it would have been technically possible, but instead of cropping from the right I would like to see more from the left side. Then there also could be seen more of the darker area on the left, which corresponds the the right side. Lighting: I guess the red colour comes from a wrong or no white balance setting. I don''t know of that was intentional but I would like it be more "natural". I know it''s a bit silly because for this subject it does not matter if it''s red, yellow, blue or green. But the red just _looks_ like a technical "error". While testing some different crops I also changed the hue a little (+16) which results in a more golden/yellow tone. I think that''s much better.The "star" effect created by the small aperture is nice. Focus: Generally ok. 1/2s exposure time, so you surely used a tripod. I can''t make out where the camera focussed, but the foliage could be a bit sharper. Challenge: Met. A deeper tunnel would have had more impact, but it certainly fits to the challenge theme. Creativity: Well, not overly creative, but not totally boring either. If you have questions just send me an Email. I''ll happily discuss this more.-Stephan', '2003-02-22 22:21:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(221516, 12285, 3087, '<td valign="top">JK Just to let you know that I have drawn your image from the CC and will be adding a critique today or tomorrow.<br/>Kavey</td>', 'JK Just to let you know that I have drawn your image from the CC and will be adding a critique today or tomorrow.Kavey', '2003-02-23 06:57:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(221522, 12207, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil (who happens to be 3otherboysdad :))<br/> <br/>Composition - I like the idea of this pic, it works well for the challenge.  Biggest most distracting thing about this picture is the top right corner where the ceiling is showing.  I think if the pic could''ve been taken from a bit higher perspective pointing down that could''ve been minimized or avoided all together.  It isn''t terribly distracting, but the first thing I noticed.  The "e" being a little cut off I think is less of a distraction.  It meets the challenge and is pretty well composed.<br/> <br/>Technical - Great job with this photo.  It is remarkably clear, well exposed, good DOF with terrific color and very crisp.  Not much else to say except that this photo is remarkably well taken.<br/> <br/>Overall - Nothing blowing me away, nothing flashy, but an excellent idea and very well executed.  The things that I mentioned that could be improved are easily fixable (easy for me to say :) but I don''t think would''ve made your score jump up by leaps and bounds, because this is a good photo regardless.  Nice job and keep the good photos coming.  Good luck with DPC and your boys. - Inspzil</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil (who happens to be 3otherboysdad :)) Composition - I like the idea of this pic, it works well for the challenge.  Biggest most distracting thing about this picture is the top right corner where the ceiling is showing.  I think if the pic could''ve been taken from a bit higher perspective pointing down that could''ve been minimized or avoided all together.  It isn''t terribly distracting, but the first thing I noticed.  The "e" being a little cut off I think is less of a distraction.  It meets the challenge and is pretty well composed. Technical - Great job with this photo.  It is remarkably clear, well exposed, good DOF with terrific color and very crisp.  Not much else to say except that this photo is remarkably well taken. Overall - Nothing blowing me away, nothing flashy, but an excellent idea and very well executed.  The things that I mentioned that could be improved are easily fixable (easy for me to say :) but I don''t think would''ve made your score jump up by leaps and bounds, because this is a good photo regardless.  Nice job and keep the good photos coming.  Good luck with DPC and your boys. - Inspzil', '2003-02-23 09:23:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(221614, 12285, 3087, '<td valign="top"><b>Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts</b> <br/>Great eye contact and wonderful textures and colours.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content</b> <br/>I really like the angles of the lines of coloured scales, the snakes mouth and the lower coil of his body within the frame. I like that we are slightly sideways on to his face but not overly so. <br/>Colour-wise I love the limited brown, beige, cream and orangey colour palette.<br/>I am in two minds about the DOF choice  I love that the background is out of focus while the eye is so wonderfully sharp but I am not sure about the nose/ tip of his face being out of focus. <br/>In terms of focal point my eye is torn between the clearly focussed eye and the fuzzy area that is the nose. <br/>I guess the only way to know for sure would be to see both choices  did you happen to take it with more DOF too by any chance?<br/> <br/><b>Background</b> <br/>Perfectly out of focus and the orangey colour is just right.<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work - Technical</b> <br/>Wondefully sharp. Very well captured.<br/> <br/><b>Digital Processing - Technical</b> <br/>Great crop (if this isnt the shot straight out of the camera) and no artifacts visible (on my monitor anyway).<br/> <br/><b>Fits The Challenge</b> <br/>Definitely.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo</b> <br/>Compelling.<br/> <br/> <br/> <br/><b>Questions</b> <br/>Did you take it through glass? It doesnt seem so.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-02-23 13:23:35.</i></td>', 'Critique Club   Initial thoughts Great eye contact and wonderful textures and colours. Composition/ Content I really like the angles of the lines of coloured scales, the snakes mouth and the lower coil of his body within the frame. I like that we are slightly sideways on to his face but not overly so. Colour-wise I love the limited brown, beige, cream and orangey colour palette.I am in two minds about the DOF choice  I love that the background is out of focus while the eye is so wonderfully sharp but I am not sure about the nose/ tip of his face being out of focus. In terms of focal point my eye is torn between the clearly focussed eye and the fuzzy area that is the nose. I guess the only way to know for sure would be to see both choices  did you happen to take it with more DOF too by any chance? Background Perfectly out of focus and the orangey colour is just right. Camera Work - Technical Wondefully sharp. Very well captured. Digital Processing - Technical Great crop (if this isnt the shot straight out of the camera) and no artifacts visible (on my monitor anyway). Fits The Challenge Definitely. My Opinion On The Photo Compelling.   Questions Did you take it through glass? It doesnt seem so.', '2003-02-23 13:23:14.000000', '2003-02-23 13:23:35.000000', 0),
(221641, 12288, 4493, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comment:<br/> <br/>You really have a nice perspective here. The combination of the cold steel with the cool blue sky are a nice combination. Without being able to modify the perspective in post processing, this angle is good. Your decision to square the tunnel at the end works well. My eye is drawn to it and it makes me wonder whats beyond. Your technical skill is good. There are no artifacts or over sharpening problems. I like the colors here, they make me feel small within this structure. I''m not sure a square crop was the best decision here, but you probably chose it to exclude some undesirable building on the right.<br/> <br/>You have a strong shot here! Good Job!<br/> <br/>-danny</td>', 'Critique Club Comment: You really have a nice perspective here. The combination of the cold steel with the cool blue sky are a nice combination. Without being able to modify the perspective in post processing, this angle is good. Your decision to square the tunnel at the end works well. My eye is drawn to it and it makes me wonder whats beyond. Your technical skill is good. There are no artifacts or over sharpening problems. I like the colors here, they make me feel small within this structure. I''m not sure a square crop was the best decision here, but you probably chose it to exclude some undesirable building on the right. You have a strong shot here! Good Job! -danny', '2003-02-23 14:16:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(221718, 12129, 2337, '<td valign="top"><i><b>~ Critique Club Comment ~</b></i> <br/> <br/><b>Composition : </b>Certainly an interesting perspective.  I would have liked to see this with a few different rotations.  My eye really wants to see one of the edges straight (top bottom or a side).  But I like the effect you achieved here.  I think the lack of an anchoring line might add to the dizzying effect.<br/> <br/><b>Exposure / Lighting : </b>As was pointed out already, it does seem a bit dark.  While it works well as is for pure lines and geometry, I think a tad more brightness may have made it a stunning shot.<br/> <br/><b>Focus : </b>Focus is not an issue.<br/> <br/><b>Post Processing : </b>May have benefited from a touch more brightness.  <br/> <br/><b>Challenge / Wow : </b>The challenge was certainly met here.  A different rotation may (or may not) have helped give greater WoW.  I feel strongly that a tad more brightness would have been an improvement.</td>', '~ Critique Club Comment ~  Composition : Certainly an interesting perspective.  I would have liked to see this with a few different rotations.  My eye really wants to see one of the edges straight (top bottom or a side).  But I like the effect you achieved here.  I think the lack of an anchoring line might add to the dizzying effect. Exposure / Lighting : As was pointed out already, it does seem a bit dark.  While it works well as is for pure lines and geometry, I think a tad more brightness may have made it a stunning shot. Focus : Focus is not an issue. Post Processing : May have benefited from a touch more brightness.   Challenge / Wow : The challenge was certainly met here.  A different rotation may (or may not) have helped give greater WoW.  I feel strongly that a tad more brightness would have been an improvement.', '2003-02-23 18:51:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(221803, 12204, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>(I apologize for this being so late; my internet connection has been bonkers the past few days, and I am just now able to connect long enough to do it! :/ )<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION<br/>You have used the rule of thirds very well.  The flag is positioned on teh left third in such a way to anchor the shot, and the angle of the flag pole is very effective at drawing the eyes up and through the picture.  I almost think you could have cropped the moon out, though it is a nice touch, because it is so close to the bottom of the frame, it looks kinda like an accident, and leaves me feeling a bit unbalanced, as  the flag is so much bigger than it.<br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE<br/>Wonderful use of lighting here.  The colors are so "pure" and rich; very nicely done.  Taking pictures of flags is difficult, as they don''t want to stand still, but you have nailed the technique, I believe.  I can think of nothing to do to improve it.<br/> <br/>OVERALL EFFECT<br/>You have done very well here, and have captured the majesty of the flag well.  It is bold and strong, and a shot to be proud of.  Nicely done!</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat (I apologize for this being so late; my internet connection has been bonkers the past few days, and I am just now able to connect long enough to do it! :/ ) COMPOSITIONYou have used the rule of thirds very well.  The flag is positioned on teh left third in such a way to anchor the shot, and the angle of the flag pole is very effective at drawing the eyes up and through the picture.  I almost think you could have cropped the moon out, though it is a nice touch, because it is so close to the bottom of the frame, it looks kinda like an accident, and leaves me feeling a bit unbalanced, as  the flag is so much bigger than it. TECHNIQUEWonderful use of lighting here.  The colors are so "pure" and rich; very nicely done.  Taking pictures of flags is difficult, as they don''t want to stand still, but you have nailed the technique, I believe.  I can think of nothing to do to improve it. OVERALL EFFECTYou have done very well here, and have captured the majesty of the flag well.  It is bold and strong, and a shot to be proud of.  Nicely done!', '2003-02-23 21:30:02.000000', NULL, 0),
